        ("Inheritance and polymorphism", "STUDY METHOD\nStart with an is-a relationship, then verify it from the business domain rather than reusing code alone.\n\nCORE CONTENT\nInheritance shares implementation and an interface. Prefer composition for unrelated behavior. Override only when substituting a subtype preserves the base contract. Polymorphism dispatches behavior at runtime.\n\nPRACTICE\nModel Payment, CardPayment, and BankPayment. Add a single process method and call it through the base type.\n\nJOB-READY CHECK\nCompare inheritance and composition and explain Liskov substitution."),
        ("Interfaces and abstraction", "STUDY METHOD\nSeparate 'what callers need' from 'how a component implements it'.\n\nCORE CONTENT\nInterfaces define contracts. They reduce coupling and allow alternate implementations. An abstract class shares partial implementation; a Java interface expresses a capability or behavior.\n\nPRACTICE\nCreate an OrderRepository interface with in-memory and file implementations. Let service code depend on the interface.\n\nJOB-READY CHECK\nExplain dependency inversion and how interfaces make testing easier."),
        ("Collections and generics", "STUDY METHOD\nChoose a collection from access, uniqueness, and iteration needs.\n\nCORE CONTENT\nList preserves order, Set removes duplicates, and Map indexes values by keys. ArrayList is usually the default List; HashMap is a common Map. Generics provide compile-time type safety.\n\nPRACTICE\nRead records into a Map<String, Integer>, find duplicates, sort a copy, and iterate safely.\n\nJOB-READY CHECK\nCompare ArrayList and LinkedList and explain when List.equals can differ from Set.equals."),
        ("Exceptions and resources", "STUDY METHOD\nSeparate expected domain outcomes from unexpected failures.\n\nCORE CONTENT\nUnchecked exceptions usually indicate programming or system failures. Checked exceptions require explicit handling but can create noisy APIs. Close resources with try-with-resources and preserve context with chained exceptions.\n\nPRACTICE\nBuild a CSV reader that closes files automatically and converts low-level errors into a clear custom exception.\n\nJOB-READY CHECK\nExplain checked versus unchecked exceptions and why catching Exception broadly is risky."),
        ("Clean code and testing", "STUDY METHOD\nWrite the behavior, then the test, then refactor. Each test should prove one contract.\n\nCORE CONTENT\nUse descriptive names, short methods, one responsibility, and predictable formatting. JUnit 5 tests arrange, act, and assert. Test boundaries and failure states, not implementation details.\n\nPRACTICE\nWrite JUnit tests for a shopping cart. Introduce no mocks initially; test pure domain behavior directly.\n\nJOB-READY CHECK\nDefine unit, integration, and end-to-end tests and explain why tests are business safeguards."),
